## 1. Introduction

Double white dwarf (WD) binaries are formed via common envelope evolution in binary systems. The emission of gravitational waves in the closest double WD binaries shrinks their orbits and causes mass transfer between the WDs within a Hubble timescale. The resulting interaction leads to a broad range of outcomes including explosive transient phenomena and the formation of exotic classes of stars.

Previous work has found that the dynamical stability of mass transfer in these binaries, and thus their ultimate fates, depends on the mass ratio of the donor mass to the accretor mass, q=M_{2}/M_{1}, and on the timescale for angular momentum feedback from the accretor to the orbit (Marsh et al., 2004; Kremer et al., 2015). However, systems are generally assumed to be dynamically stable if accretion occurs via a disk, as torques on the extended lever arm of the disk should yield efficient angular momentum feedback. In this Letter, we challenge this assumption by showing that even disk-accreting double WD binaries with extremely low-mass He WD donors will merge unstably.

The overlying non-degenerate H-rich layers surrounding these low-mass WDs alters the initial phase of mass transfer compared to simplified pure He WD donors (D'Antona et al., 2006; Kaplan et al., 2012) and leads to classical nova-like outbursts (Shen et al., 2013). Subsequent accretion of the underlying He-rich WD core yields analogous He-powered nova episodes (Shen & Bildsten, 2009). Here, we consider the impact of these expanding nova shells on the binary orbit. We calculate the evolution of two fiducial double WD binaries with masses 0.2+0.5 and 0.2+1.0M_{\\odot} and find that these binaries, which were previously assumed to form stably mass transferring AM Canum Venaticorum systems (Kilic et al., 2014), instead merge due to dynamical friction within the accretor's expanded outer layers. In Section 2, we estimate the shrinking of the orbital separation due to mass ejection via dynamical friction within the common envelope formed by the nova shell. In Section 3, we provide a prescription for how the donor's mass transfer rate responds to the decrease in orbital separation. In Section 4, we describe our fiducial calculations, and we consider the implications of our findings in Section 5.

## 2. Common envelope energetics

We begin by estimating the change in the binary separation, a, if some or all of the energy to eject the expanding nova shell comes from the orbit via dynamical friction of the two WDs with the common envelope. This is at best a rough approximation (see Ivanova et al. 2013 and references therein); however, we use it here for simplicity and defer analysis with alternative common envelope physics to future work.

The specific energy required to eject material to infinity from the Roche surface of the accreting WD is

\\Phi_{\\rm L1} = -\\frac{GM_{\\rm t}}{a}\\left[\\frac{1/(1+q)}{1-x_{\\rm L1}}+\\frac{q/( 1+q)}{x_{\\rm L1}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{1+q}-x_{\\rm L1}\\right)^{2}\\right]  \\equiv -\\frac{GM_{\\rm t}}{a}f(q), where the total mass of the system is M_{\\rm t}=M_{1}+M_{2}, and x_{\\rm L1} is the distance from the center of the donor to the L_{1} Lagrange point in units of the binary separation. The function f(q) varies from 1.4-1.9 for0.5. While this specific energy changes slightly as mass is being ejected from the system, it will remain correct to first order in the ratio of the ejecta mass, M_{\\rm ej}, to the total mass and thus is sufficient for our purposes of estimation.

Assuming that a fraction \\alpha_{\\rm CE} of the energy to eject M_{\\rm ej} comes from the binding energy of the orbit, we obtain the change in binary separation following the common envelope event,

\\frac{da}{a}=-\\frac{M_{\\rm ej}}{M_{1}}\\left[\\alpha_{\\rm CE}\\frac{2(1+q)f(q)}{q }+1\\right].  which is again correct to first order in (M_{\\rm ej}/M_{t}). Note that because of the factor of 2/q, the ratio da/a can be more than an order of magnitude larger than M_{\\rm ej}/M_{1}.

## 3. Prescriptions for the Accretion Rate

Dynamical friction during the common envelope event reduces the binary separation, and thus increases the Roche overfill factor, \\Delta=R_{2}-R_{\\rm L2}, where R_{2} is the radius of the donor star, and R_{\\rm L2} is the donor's Roche radius, which is approximated by Paczynski (1967) as

R_{\\rm L2}=0.46a\\left(\\frac{M_{2}}{M_{t}}\\right)^{1/3}. 

As a result of the increased overfill factor, the accretion rate rises. In order to estimate the new perturbed accretion rate, we require a relation for \\dot{M}_{2}(\\Delta). Several such prescriptions exist, including the isothermal approximation (Ritter, 1988); however, as pointed out by Marsh et al. (2004), this approximation breaks down when \\Delta is larger than the pressure scale height at the donor's photosphere, which will indeed be the case for our post-nova binaries.

We therefore use the adiabatic \\dot{M} prescription (Paczynski & Sienkiewicz, 1972; Savonije, 1978; Webbink, 1984; Marsh et al., 2004), which assumes an equation of state P=K\\rho^{\\Gamma_{1}} with constant K and \\Gamma_{1} along equipotential surfaces. However, because we are interested in the early phase of mass transfer involving the donor's outer, non-degenerate layers, we cannot assume a constant polytropic index for all donor material as the previously cited studies do. Instead, we follow the more general expression of Ge et al. (2010), which allows for a varying polytropic index within the donor at the complicating expense of requiring a stellar structure integral. Their approximation to \\dot{M}_{2}(\\Delta) is

\\dot{M}_{2}(\\Delta)\\sim 2\\pi R_{2}^{2}h(q)\\int_{1-\\Delta/R_{2}}^{1}\\left(\\frac{ 2}{\\Gamma_{1}+1}\\right)^{\\frac{\\Gamma_{1}+1}{2(\\Gamma_{1}-1)}}c_{s}\\rho dx  where c_{s} is the sound speed, the factor h(q) is

h(q)=\\frac{q}{\\left(R_{\\rm L2}/a\\right)^{3}(1+q)}\\frac{1}{\\left[a_{2}(a_{2}-1) \\right]^{1/2}},  with a_{2} given as

a_{2}=\\frac{q/\\left(1+q\\right)}{x_{\\rm L1}^{3}}+\\frac{1/\\left(1+q\\right)}{ \\left(1-x_{\\rm L1}\\right)^{3}},  and we have assumed \\Delta\\ll R_{2}. The function h(q) ranges from 1.7-1.4 for q=0.1-0.5.

## 4. Fiducial Examples

We now utilize the relations in Sections 2 and 3 to calculate the evolution of two fiducial double WD binaries consisting of an extremely low-mass 0.2 M_{\\odot} He WD donor and 0.5 and 1.0 M_{\\odot} C/O WD accretors. The WDs are constructed and evolved with the stellar evolution code MESA2(Paxton et al., 2011, 2013), beginning with initial metallicities of 0.02 and main sequence masses of 0.8 and 7 M_{\\odot} for the 0.5 and 1.0 M_{\\odot} C/O WD models, respectively. These WDs are taken from the set of default WD models included with MESA, which employ Reimers (1975) and Blocker (1995) winds to remove mass on the red giant and asymptotic giant branches, leaving hot degenerate cores. We begin with these cores, turn on chemical diffusion, and allow them to cool for 10^{10} yr to approximate the long main sequence timescale of the low-mass companion that will give rise to the 0.2 M_{\\odot} He WD.

