### The binary's response to the expanding accretor

The \\dot{M}_{2} history, including the changing accreted composition, is then used to evolve the accreting WDs. Chemical diffusion is active in these calculations as before. After 1.8\\times 10^{-3}~{}M_{\\odot} has been transferred to the 0.5~{}M_{\\odot} accretor, nuclear burning triggers convection in the outer 2.8\\times 10^{-3}~{}M_{\\odot}, and a classical nova-like outburst powered by H-burning begins. As we will discuss in Section 4.3, the 0.2+1.0~{}M_{\\odot} binary will likely remain dynamically stable after its first H-powered outbursts. We thus instead begin the accretor's evolution for this binary after the initial H-rich 0.007~{}M_{\\odot} has already been transferred and the accreted material transitions to >0.99 He by mass. Convection then begins on the 1.0~{}M_{\\odot} accretor after an additional 3.3\\times 10^{-3}~{}M_{\\odot} of He-rich material is accreted, resulting in an expanding envelope of 2.9\\times 10^{-3}~{}M_{\\odot}. Bullets and squares in Figure 1 show the accretion rates and Roche overflow factors for the first H-powered and He-powered novae, respectively.

As the convective layer heats and expands, it overflows the accretor's Roche lobe and forms a common envelope surrounding the binary. Even though the majority of the convective layer is expelled, the ejection of even a small fraction of the shell via dynamical friction is sufficient to increase the donor's Roche overfill factor and consequently \\dot{M}_{2} by a tremendous amount.

Figure 2 shows the donor's \\dot{M}_{2}(\\Delta) relation at the moment when convection is initiated on the accretor. Diamonds demarcate \\dot{M}_{2}(\\Delta_{0}) just prior to the common envelope event. The overfill factors resulting from the decreased orbital separation, assuming \\alpha_{\\rm CE}=0.1 and 1.0, and the corresponding mass transfer rates are shown as triangles and squares, respectively. It is clear that the ejection of a relatively small envelope via a common envelope has a drastic effect on these systems, resulting in accretion rates that are highly super-Eddington with respect to the accretors; the Eddington accretion rates, \\dot{M}_{\\rm Edd}, are shown as crosses.

### The post-common envelope evolution

We now calculate the evolution of our fiducial systems in their post-common envelope, super-Eddington accretion states. To simplify matters, we approximate the donor's \\dot{M}_{2}(\\Delta) relation as a power-law: \\dot{M}_{2}\\propto\\Delta^{b}. A value of b=3 is appropriate for purely degenerate objects, as utilized by Marsh et al. (2004) and others. Even though the donors in our fiducial binaries are not fully degenerate, the results in Figure 2 imply that b\\simeq 3 in these cases as well. With this simplification, the evolution of \\dot{M}_{2} can be written as

\\frac{d\\dot{M}_{2}/dt}{\\dot{M}_{2}}\\sim\\frac{bR_{2}}{\\Delta}\\left[\\left(\\xi_{ \\rm rapid}-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\frac{\\dot{M}_{2}}{M_{2}}-\\frac{\\dot{a}}{a}+ \\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\dot{M}_{t}}{M_{t}}\\right]. 

We have assumed that \\Delta\\ll R_{2} and used the Paczynski (1967) Roche lobe relation. The donor's radial response to the extremely rapid mass loss that follows the common envelope event is \\xi_{\\rm rapid}.

Because the post-common envelope accretion rate is so highly super-Eddington, we further assume that \\dot{M}_{t}=\\dot{M}_{2}. Following a similar energetic argument to that used in Section 2, we find

\\frac{\\dot{a}}{a}=\\frac{\\dot{M}_{2}}{M_{2}}\\left[2\\alpha_{\\rm CE}(1+q)f(q)+1 \\right], 

